# Byte-faithful checkouts everywhere (round 296).
#
# Git-for-Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which smudges every
# file git sniffs as text LF->CRLF in the working tree. The Windows
# release runner (and any clean Windows machine the Stage-5 smoke test
# will use) therefore received CRLF-mangled bytes, and the toolkit's
# own gates caught it exactly as designed:
#
#   * test_database_audit's structural integrity guard: "CR bytes
#     present" in cellline_expression.csv;
#   * the seed_molecules.csv byte pin: 455 B on disk vs the 443 B the
#     index carries (exactly 12 lines x 1 CR);
#   * the docking-campaign tally: every size-pinned r.dlg (682 B)
#     grew past its pin and fell out of "OK" into "UNEXPECTED";
#   * the data builder's --check drift gate: the regenerated (LF)
#     bytes stopped matching the smudged working tree.
#
# Marking every path `-text` disables the EOL smudge/clean filters for
# all files on every platform: the working tree is byte-identical to
# the index, which is what every byte-pinned guard in this repository
# assumes and what the shipped archive must contain. The repository is
# authored LF-only from Linux, so there is nothing to normalize and
# nothing to renormalize -- verified by scanning the index for CR
# bytes (only the binary ZIP archives in dist-archive/ contain 0x0D,
# and those are untouched by -text).
* -text
